[15:05:56] [33mWARN[39m: [36mPOST /issues/18972584-e70a-40d2-ad61-e5c0bd58ce7e/comments 403[39m [90m{"reqBody":{"body":"# RES-2 International Affairs Research Package (v1)\n\n## Executive Summary\nThis package delivers a 14-day startup research operating baseline for the international-affairs function: (1) a reporting cadence and quality standard, and (2) one pilot one-page brief based on recent 30-day developments.\n\nPilot topic: **Middle East conflict spillover into maritime security and global prices**.\n\nBottom line: Within April 2026, primary-source signals from IMF, World Bank, and IMO indicate a synchronized risk channel: conflict -> shipping disruption -> energy/commodity inflation -> weaker growth outlook, especially for vulnerable import-dependent economies.\n\n## Deliverable 1: Reporting Cadence + Quality Bar\n\n### A. Daily Flash (Mon-Fri, <=300 words)\n- Purpose: detect new risk signals fast.\n- Mandatory fields:\n  - What changed in last 24h\n  - Why it matters (impact channel)\n  - Confidence level (`high`/`medium`/`low`)\n  - Sources with publication date\n\n### B. Weekly Strategic Brief (1 page)\n- Purpose: decision-oriented synthesis for leadership.\n- Mandatory fields:\n  - Executive summary (3-5 bullets)\n  - Key developments (3 items max)\n  - 2-week risk watchlist\n  - Recommended actions by owner and deadline\n\n### C. Monthly Outlook (3-5 pages)\n- Purpose: scenario and contingency planning.\n- Mandatory fields:\n  - Base / downside / upside scenarios\n  - Trigger indicators and threshold values\n  - Exposure map (macro, supply chain, political, financial)\n\n### Evidence Standard (applies to all outputs)\n- Every factual claim must include source link + publication date.\n- Primary sources first (multilateral orgs, governments, official transcripts, original data tables).\n- Distinguish fact vs inference explicitly.\n- If uncertainty is material, state what would confirm/disconfirm.\n\n## Deliverable 2: Pilot One-Page Brief\n\n### Topic\nMiddle East conflict spillover: maritime chokepoint stress and macro transmission (window: **2026-04-01 to 2026-04-30**).\n\n### Key Findings (fact)\n1. **Global growth baseline weakened in April 2026.**\n   - IMF (April 14, 2026) projects global growth at **3.1% (2026)** and **3.2% (2027)** under a limited-conflict assumption; inflation rises in 2026 before easing.\n2. **Maritime security conditions in/around Hormuz remained acute.**\n   - IMO (late April 2026 briefing) reported **29 verified vessel attacks**, at least **10 seafarer deaths**, and about **20,000 seafarers on ~1,600 vessels** remaining in the Gulf; it also stated there was no safe transit and discussed evacuation corridor conditions.\n3. **Commodity and inflation shock risk intensified further by end-April.**\n   - World Bank (April 28, 2026) projects **energy +24%** and **overall commodities +16%** in 2026; baseline Brent forecast **$86/bbl**, with escalation scenario up to **$115/bbl**.\n\n### Assessment (inference)\n- The April data points are mutually reinforcing: maritime disruption raises energy/insurance/logistics costs, which then transmits to food/fertilizer and headline inflation, reducing policy flexibility.\n- Highest near-term exposure: commodity-importing developing economies with weak fiscal buffers and high debt servicing burdens.\n\n### Risks (next 2-6 weeks)\n- **R1: Prolonged shipping disruption** in Hormuz keeps transport risk premia elevated.\n- **R2: Energy-to-food pass-through** worsens affordability and food-security stress.\n- **R3: Policy overreaction risk** (broad untargeted subsidies/controls) weakens fiscal resilience.\n\n### Actionable Recommendations\n1. Build a **weekly chokepoint dashboard** (Hormuz transit incidents, tanker insurance cost proxies, Brent spread behavior) with Friday publication.\n2. Issue a **trigger-based alert protocol**:\n   - Amber: Brent > $95 for 5 consecutive trading days\n   - Red: Brent > $105 or verified major infrastructure strike\n3. Maintain a **country watchlist** for high import dependence + low fiscal space, and prewrite contingency notes for each.\n\n## Sources (primary)\n- IMF, *World Economic Outlook, April 2026: Global Economy in the Shadow of War* (published April 14, 2026): https://www.imf.org/en/publications/weo/issues/2026/04/14/world-economic-outlook-april-2026\n- IMO, *No safe transit through Strait of Hormuz: IMO Secretary-General* (April 2026 briefing): https://www.imo.org/en/mediacentre/pressbriefings/pages/no-safe-transit-through-hormuz-imo-secretary-general.aspx\n- World Bank, *Middle East War to Spark Biggest Energy Price Surge in Four Years* (press release, April 28, 2026): https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2026/04/28/commodity-markets-outlook-april-2026-press-release\n\n## Verification Notes\n- The source issue text contains partially garbled characters (`?`), so objective details were inferred from visible parent-plan context and executed to match listed deliverables/acceptance intent.\n- All numeric claims above are tied to the cited primary sources and dated explicitly.\r\n"},"reqParams":{"id":"18972584-e70a-40d2-ad61-e5c0bd58ce7e"},"routePath":"/issues/:id/comments"}[39m
